<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Sample Past Exam 2, with solutions.

  Transcribed from the instructor's annotated solutions PDF of the Winter
  2025 second exam (MATH_14_Exam__2_W2025_-_solutions.pdf): questions 1, 2,
  3 and 5 with the handwritten solutions worked out in full.  The exam's
  optional sixth question, on a line integral, is left out at the
  instructor's request; question 6 here is the true/false problem of the
  instructor's triple_integral_true_false_solution.pdf (the integral of
  x^2/(x^2+y^2+z^2) over the unit ball), set in the exam's true/false
  format with the rule "prove it if true, give a counterexample if
  false", and its solution transcribed.  Question 4 of that exam (the circle in a horizontal field) is
  already question 6 of Sample Past Exam 1, so in its place stands question
  1 of the Winter 2025 third exam (MATH_14_Exam__3_W2025_solutions.pdf), a
  spherical-coordinates rewrite, at the instructor's request.  Point values
  are deliberately left out.  Built like sample-past-exam-1.ptx: one
  exercise per question, a task per part, a solution under each, and the
  "sec-exercises-" id prefix, so assets/unnumbered-chapter.css hides its
  number and xsl/custom-html.xsl keeps its solutions behind knowls (it is
  a worksheet).  Included from main.ptx right after Review Problems #2, in the
  skeletal book and in the completed instructor book; the two copies are
  identical.

  Every solution of questions 1 to 5 carries a figure: the projection of the solid for the two
  rewrites, the solid itself and its two projections for the change of
  order, the two polar curves for the area, and the cross-section of the
  solid for the signs question.  Question 6 is a symmetry argument and
  needs none.
-->
<worksheet xml:id="sec-exercises-sample-past-exam-2" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Sample Past Exam 2</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      A sample past exam, with worked solutions. It was a closed-book exam
      of 65 minutes: no aids of one's own (formula sheets, notes, phones,
      tablets, smart watches or similar) were permitted, and students were
      asked to write their work in a neat and organized format and to fully
      justify every step. Try the exam under the same conditions before you
      look at the solutions. Its topics are those of Exam #2, with links to
      the notes:
    </p>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p><xref ref="section-double-integrals-rectangles" text="title"/></p></li>
        <li><p><xref ref="section-double-integrals-general-regions" text="title"/></p></li>
        <li><p><xref ref="section-polar-coordinates" text="title"/></p></li>
        <li><p><xref ref="section-double-integrals-polar-form" text="title"/></p></li>
        <li><p><xref ref="section-triple-integrals-rectangular" text="title"/></p></li>
        <li><p><xref ref="section-triple-integrals-cylindrical" text="title"/></p></li>
        <li><p><xref ref="section-triple-integrals-spherical" text="title"/></p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ===================== Question 1 ===================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="spe2-q1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Rewrite (do not evaluate) the following integral in cylindrical
        coordinates:
      </p>
      <me>
        \int_0^4\!\!\int_{-\sqrt{4-(y-2)^2}}^{\sqrt{4-(y-2)^2}}\!\!
        \int_0^{\sqrt{16-x^2-y^2}} xyz\,dz\,dx\,dy .
      </me>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        The limits on <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> describe the projection of the
        solid onto the <m>xy</m>-plane: <m>0 \le y \le 4</m> and
        <m>x^2 \le 4 - (y-2)^2</m>, that is, the disk
        <m>x^2 + (y-2)^2 \le 4</m> of radius <m>2</m> centered at
        <m>(0,2)</m> (<xref ref="fig-spe2-q1-disk"/>). Its boundary circle
        is <m>x^2 + y^2 = 4y</m>, in polar coordinates
        <m>r^2 = 4r\sin\theta</m>, so <m>r = 4\sin\theta</m>; the disk is
        swept out by <m>0 \le \theta \le \pi</m>, <m>0 \le r \le 4\sin\theta</m>.
      </p>
      <figure xml:id="fig-spe2-q1-disk">
        <caption>
          The projection of the solid of <xref ref="spe2-q1"/> onto the <m>xy</m>-plane: the disk <m>x^2 + (y-2)^2 \le 4</m>, whose boundary is <m>r = 4\sin\theta</m>. A ray at angle <m>\theta</m> leaves the disk at <m>r = 4\sin\theta</m>; the rays that meet it have <m>0 \le \theta \le \pi</m>.
        </caption>
        <image width="45%">
          <shortdescription>
          A shaded disk of radius two centered at the point zero comma two, tangent to the x axis at the origin, with a ray from the origin at angle theta ending on its boundary.
          </shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>
            Coordinate axes x and y. A disk of radius two, centered at the point zero comma two on the y axis, is shaded tan; its boundary circle passes through the origin and the point zero comma four. A green ray from the origin at an angle theta above the x axis ends on the circle, where r equals four sine theta, and a small arc marks the angle.
            </p>
          </description>
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-spe2-q1-disk">
            <diagram dimensions="(300,256)" margins="10">
              <definition>circ(t) = (2*cos(t), 2 + 2*sin(t))</definition>
              <coordinates bbox="(-2.8,-0.7,3.4,4.6)">
              <circle at="q1-disk" center="(0,2)" radius="2" fill="#fbe3d0" stroke="none"/>
              <axes at="q1-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" hlabels="(-2,2,2)" vlabels="(2,2,4)" arrows="1"/>
              <parametric-curve at="q1-circle" function="circ" domain="(0,2*pi)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
              <line at="q1-ray" endpoints="((0,0),(1.5321,2.5712))" stroke="#009E73" thickness="2.5"/>
              <arc at="q1-angle" center="(0,0)" radius="0.6" range="(0,59.2)" stroke="#009E73" thickness="2"/>
              <point at="q1-center" p="(0,2)" fill="black" size="3.5"/>
              <point at="q1-end" p="(1.5321,2.5712)" fill="#009E73" size="3.5"/>
              <label at="q1-thetalab" anchor="(0.75,0.28)" alignment="e" color="#009E73"><m>\theta</m></label>
              <label at="q1-rlab" anchor="(1.5321,2.5712)" alignment="e" offset="(5,0)" color="#009E73"><m>r = 4\sin\theta</m></label>
              <label at="q1-clab" anchor="(0,2)" alignment="sw" offset="(-6,-4)"><m>(0,2)</m></label>
              <label at="q1-eqlab" anchor="(0.3,4.2)" alignment="ne" color="#0072B2"><m>x^2+(y-2)^2=4</m></label>
              </coordinates>
              <annotations>
              <annotation ref="figure" text="The disk of radius two centered at zero comma two, the shadow of the solid.">
                <annotation ref="q1-disk" text="The shaded disk."/>
                <annotation ref="q1-circle" text="Its boundary circle, r equals four sine theta in polar coordinates." sonify="yes"/>
                <annotation ref="q1-ray" text="A ray from the origin at angle theta, which leaves the disk at r equals four sine theta."/>
              </annotation>
              </annotations>
            </diagram>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
      </figure>
      <p>
        The limits on <m>z</m> run from the plane <m>z = 0</m> up to
        <m>z = \sqrt{16 - x^2 - y^2} = \sqrt{16 - r^2}</m>, the upper half
        of the sphere of radius <m>4</m>. The integrand is
        <m>xyz = (r\cos\theta)(r\sin\theta)\,z = r^2 z\cos\theta\sin\theta</m>,
        and <m>dV = r\,dz\,dr\,d\theta</m> by
        <xref ref="eq-dv-cylindrical"/>. Hence
      </p>
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \int_0^4\!\!\int_{-\sqrt{4-(y-2)^2}}^{\sqrt{4-(y-2)^2}}\!\!
          \int_0^{\sqrt{16-x^2-y^2}} xyz\,dz\,dx\,dy
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \quad = \int_0^{\pi}\!\!\int_0^{4\sin\theta}\!\!\int_0^{\sqrt{16-r^2}}
          r^3 z\cos\theta\sin\theta\,dz\,dr\,d\theta .
        </mrow>
      </md>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ===================== Question 2 ===================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="spe2-q2">
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Consider the integral
        <m>\displaystyle\int_0^1\!\!\int_0^{x^2}\!\!\int_0^{y} f(x,y,z)\,dz\,dy\,dx</m>.
        Rewrite (do not evaluate) this integral in the following forms.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="spe2-q2-a">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\displaystyle\iiint_R f(x,y,z)\,dy\,dx\,dz</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The given limits describe the solid
          <me>
            R:\quad 0 \le x \le 1,\qquad 0 \le y \le x^2,\qquad 0 \le z \le y ,
          </me>
          shown in <xref ref="fig-spe2-q2-solid"/>: it stands on the
          region of the <m>xy</m>-plane between the parabola
          <m>y = x^2</m> and the <m>x</m>-axis, up to the plane
          <m>z = y</m>.
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-spe2-q2-solid">
          <caption>
            The solid <m>R</m> of <xref ref="spe2-q2"/>: over the region <m>0 \le y \le x^2</m>, <m>0 \le x \le 1</m> of the <m>xy</m>-plane, between the floor <m>z = 0</m> and the sloping plane <m>z = y</m>. Its other faces are the parabolic cylinder <m>y = x^2</m> and the plane <m>x = 1</m>.
          </caption>
          <image width="60%">
            <shortdescription>
            A wedge-shaped solid rising from the x axis: its floor is the region under the parabola y equals x squared, its curved back wall is the parabolic cylinder, its flat side is the plane x equals one, and its top is the sloping plane z equals y.
            </shortdescription>
            <description>
              <p>
              Coordinate axes x, y and z. A translucent solid stands on the region of the xy-plane between the x axis and the parabola y equals x squared, for x from zero to one. Its top is the plane z equals y, which touches the floor along the x axis and rises to height one at the point one comma one comma one. Its curved blue wall is the parabolic cylinder y equals x squared and its flat orange wall is the triangle in the plane x equals one.
              </p>
            </description>
            <latex-image>
              <![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[line join=round, line cap=round]
                        \definecolor{oiblue}{RGB}{0,114,178}
                        \definecolor{oiorange}{RGB}{213,94,0}
                        \begin{axis}[
                            view={40}{22},
                            width=10cm,
                            axis lines=center,
                            axis line style={-latex, black!70, line width=0.7pt},
                            xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
                            label style={font=\large},
                            ticks=none,
                            xmin=-0.15, xmax=1.45, ymin=-0.15, ymax=1.45, zmin=0, zmax=1.3,
                            clip=false,
                            unit vector ratio=1 1 1,
                          ]
                          % the floor z = 0: 0 <= y <= x^2
                          \addplot3[surf, shader=flat, color=oiorange!20, opacity=0.5, fill opacity=0.5,
                                    domain=0:1, samples=20, y domain=0:1, samples y=2,
                                    z buffer=sort, forget plot]
                            ({x}, {y*x^2}, 0);
                          % the curved wall y = x^2, from z = 0 up to z = y = x^2
                          \addplot3[surf, shader=faceted, faceted color=oiblue!30,
                                    colormap={wallq2}{color=(oiblue!12!white) color=(oiblue!50!white)},
                                    opacity=0.6, fill opacity=0.6,
                                    domain=0:1, samples=20, y domain=0:1, samples y=8,
                                    z buffer=sort, forget plot]
                            ({x}, {x^2}, {y*x^2});
                          % the flat wall x = 1: 0 <= z <= y
                          \addplot3[surf, shader=flat, color=oiorange!35, opacity=0.6, fill opacity=0.6,
                                    domain=0:1, samples=8, y domain=0:1, samples y=8,
                                    z buffer=sort, forget plot]
                            (1, {x}, {x*y});
                          % the top z = y
                          \addplot3[surf, shader=faceted, faceted color=oiblue!25,
                                    colormap={topq2}{color=(oiblue!15!white) color=(oiblue!45!white)},
                                    opacity=0.55, fill opacity=0.55,
                                    domain=0:1, samples=20, y domain=0:1, samples y=8,
                                    z buffer=sort, forget plot]
                            ({x}, {y*x^2}, {y*x^2});
                          % edges
                          \addplot3[black!65, dashed, line width=1pt, samples=40, samples y=0, domain=0:1, forget plot]
                            ({x}, {x^2}, 0);
                          \addplot3[oiblue, line width=1.5pt, samples=40, samples y=0, domain=0:1, forget plot]
                            ({x}, {x^2}, {x^2});
                          \draw[black!65, dashed, line width=1pt] (axis cs:1,0,0) -- (axis cs:1,1,0);
                          \draw[oiorange!85!black, line width=1.3pt] (axis cs:1,1,0) -- (axis cs:1,1,1);
                          \draw[oiblue, line width=1.5pt] (axis cs:1,0,0) -- (axis cs:1,1,1);
                          \draw[black!65, line width=1pt] (axis cs:0,0,0) -- (axis cs:1,0,0);
                          % labels
                          \node[oiblue, font=\small, anchor=south east] at (axis cs:0.55,0.3,0.55) {$z = y$};
                          \node[oiblue, font=\small, anchor=west] at (axis cs:0.75,0.56,0.1) {$y = x^2$};
                          \node[oiorange!85!black, font=\small, anchor=west] at (axis cs:1,1.08,0.5) {$x = 1$};
                          \node[font=\small, anchor=south west] at (axis cs:1,1,1) {$(1,1,1)$};
                        \end{axis}
                      \end{tikzpicture}]]>
            </latex-image>
          </image>
        </figure>
        <p>
          For the order <m>dy\,dx\,dz</m> the outer variable is <m>z</m>,
          which runs over <m>0 \le z \le 1</m> in all. For a fixed <m>z</m>
          the solid is the slice at that height: the condition
          <m>z \le y \le x^2</m> needs <m>x^2 \ge z</m>, that is,
          <m>\sqrt{z} \le x \le 1</m>, and then <m>y</m> runs from
          <m>z</m> up to <m>x^2</m>. The projection of <m>R</m> onto the
          <m>xz</m>-plane is the region under the parabola <m>z = x^2</m>
          (<xref ref="fig-spe2-q2-xz"/>), and
          <me>
            \iiint_R f(x,y,z)\,dy\,dx\,dz
            = \int_0^1\!\!\int_{\sqrt z}^{1}\!\!\int_{z}^{x^2} f(x,y,z)\,dy\,dx\,dz .
          </me>
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-spe2-q2-xz">
          <caption>
            The projection of <m>R</m> onto the <m>xz</m>-plane: the region <m>0 \le z \le x^2</m>, <m>0 \le x \le 1</m>, under the parabola <m>z = x^2</m>. For a fixed height <m>z</m>, <m>x</m> runs from <m>\sqrt z</m> to <m>1</m>.
          </caption>
          <image width="40%">
            <shortdescription>
            The region under the parabola z equals x squared between x equals zero and x equals one, shaded, with a horizontal strip at height z from x equals root z to x equals one.
            </shortdescription>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-spe2-q2-xz">
              <diagram dimensions="(260,252)" margins="10">
                <definition>zero(x) = 0</definition>
                <definition>par(x) = x^2</definition>
                <definition>pc(t) = (t, t^2)</definition>
                <coordinates bbox="(-0.25,-0.25,1.35,1.3)">
                <area-between-curves at="q2xz-region" function1="zero" function2="par" domain="(0,1)" fill="#fbe3d0" stroke="none"/>
                <axes at="q2xz-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="z" hlabels="(1,1,1)" vlabels="(1,1,1)" arrows="1"/>
                <parametric-curve at="q2xz-par" function="pc" domain="(0,1.1)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
                <line at="q2xz-right" endpoints="((1,0),(1,1))" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
                <line at="q2xz-strip" endpoints="((0.6325,0.4),(1,0.4))" stroke="#009E73" thickness="2.5"/>
                <point at="q2xz-s1" p="(0.6325,0.4)" fill="#009E73" size="3"/>
                <point at="q2xz-s2" p="(1,0.4)" fill="#009E73" size="3"/>
                <point at="q2xz-corner" p="(1,1)" fill="black" size="3.5"/>
                <label at="q2xz-parlab" anchor="(0.55,0.75)" alignment="nw" color="#0072B2"><m>z = x^2</m></label>
                <label at="q2xz-striplab" anchor="(0.6325,0.4)" alignment="w" offset="(-4,0)" color="#009E73"><m>x=\sqrt z</m></label>
                <label at="q2xz-x1lab" anchor="(1,0.4)" alignment="e" offset="(4,0)" color="#009E73"><m>x=1</m></label>
                <label at="q2xz-cornerlab" anchor="(1,1)" alignment="ne" offset="(3,3)"><m>(1,1)</m></label>
                </coordinates>
                <annotations>
                <annotation ref="figure" text="The projection of the solid onto the xz-plane: the region under the parabola z equals x squared from x equals zero to one.">
                  <annotation ref="q2xz-region" text="The shaded region."/>
                  <annotation ref="q2xz-par" text="The parabola z equals x squared." sonify="yes"/>
                  <annotation ref="q2xz-strip" text="A horizontal strip at height z, from x equals root z to x equals one."/>
                </annotation>
                </annotations>
              </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="spe2-q2-b">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\displaystyle\iiint_R f(x,y,z)\,dx\,dz\,dy</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Now the outer variable is <m>y</m>, with <m>0 \le y \le 1</m> in
          all; for a fixed <m>y</m>, <m>z</m> runs from <m>0</m> to
          <m>y</m>, and the inner variable <m>x</m> must satisfy
          <m>y \le x^2</m>, that is, <m>\sqrt{y} \le x \le 1</m>. The
          projection of <m>R</m> onto the <m>yz</m>-plane is the triangle
          <m>0 \le z \le y \le 1</m> (<xref ref="fig-spe2-q2-yz"/>), and
          <me>
            \iiint_R f(x,y,z)\,dx\,dz\,dy
            = \int_0^1\!\!\int_{0}^{y}\!\!\int_{\sqrt y}^{1} f(x,y,z)\,dx\,dz\,dy .
          </me>
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-spe2-q2-yz">
          <caption>
            The projection of <m>R</m> onto the <m>yz</m>-plane: the triangle <m>0 \le z \le y</m>, <m>0 \le y \le 1</m>, under the line <m>z = y</m>. For a fixed <m>y</m>, <m>z</m> runs from <m>0</m> to <m>y</m>; above each of its points, <m>x</m> runs from <m>\sqrt y</m> to <m>1</m>.
          </caption>
          <image width="40%">
            <shortdescription>
            A shaded triangle with vertices at the origin, one comma zero, and one comma one, under the line z equals y, with a vertical strip at a fixed y from z equals zero to z equals y.
            </shortdescription>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-spe2-q2-yz">
              <diagram dimensions="(260,252)" margins="10">
                <coordinates bbox="(-0.25,-0.25,1.35,1.3)">
                <polygon at="q2yz-region" points="((0,0),(1,0),(1,1))" fill="#fbe3d0" stroke="none" closed="yes"/>
                <axes at="q2yz-axes" xlabel="y" ylabel="z" hlabels="(1,1,1)" vlabels="(1,1,1)" arrows="1"/>
                <line at="q2yz-diag" endpoints="((0,0),(1.15,1.15))" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
                <line at="q2yz-right" endpoints="((1,0),(1,1))" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
                <line at="q2yz-strip" endpoints="((0.6,0),(0.6,0.6))" stroke="#009E73" thickness="2.5"/>
                <point at="q2yz-s1" p="(0.6,0)" fill="#009E73" size="3"/>
                <point at="q2yz-s2" p="(0.6,0.6)" fill="#009E73" size="3"/>
                <point at="q2yz-corner" p="(1,1)" fill="black" size="3.5"/>
                <label at="q2yz-diaglab" anchor="(0.45,0.62)" alignment="nw" color="#0072B2"><m>z = y</m></label>
                <label at="q2yz-ylab" anchor="(0.6,0)" alignment="s" offset="(0,-4)" color="#009E73"><m>y</m></label>
                <label at="q2yz-cornerlab" anchor="(1,1)" alignment="ne" offset="(3,3)"><m>(1,1)</m></label>
                </coordinates>
                <annotations>
                <annotation ref="figure" text="The projection of the solid onto the yz-plane: the triangle under the line z equals y from y equals zero to one.">
                  <annotation ref="q2yz-region" text="The shaded triangle."/>
                  <annotation ref="q2yz-diag" text="The line z equals y." sonify="yes"/>
                  <annotation ref="q2yz-strip" text="A vertical strip at a fixed y, from z equals zero to z equals y."/>
                </annotation>
                </annotations>
              </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ===================== Question 3 ===================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="spe2-q3">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Set up (do not evaluate) a double integral for the purpose of
        computing the area <em>outside</em>
        <m>\left(x^2+y^2\right)^{3/2} = 2x^2</m> and <em>inside</em>
        <m>(x-1)^2 + y^2 = 1</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Both curves are simplest in polar coordinates. Since
        <m>x^2 + y^2 = r^2</m>, the first is <m>r^3 = 2r^2\cos^2\theta</m>,
        that is, <m>r = 2\cos^2\theta</m>. The second is
        <m>x^2 + y^2 = 2x</m>, that is, <m>r^2 = 2r\cos\theta</m>, the
        circle <m>r = 2\cos\theta</m> of radius <m>1</m> centered at
        <m>(1,0)</m>. Replacing <m>\theta</m> by <m>-\theta</m> changes
        neither equation, so both curves are symmetric about the
        <m>x</m>-axis, and both are traced once as <m>\theta</m> runs from
        <m>-\pi/2</m> to <m>\pi/2</m>: a few values of
        <m>r = 2\cos^2\theta</m> are <m>2</m> at <m>\theta = 0</m>,
        <m>3/2</m> at <m>\pi/6</m>, <m>1</m> at <m>\pi/4</m>, <m>1/2</m> at
        <m>\pi/3</m>, and <m>0</m> at <m>\pi/2</m>
        (<xref ref="fig-spe2-q3-region"/>).
      </p>
      <figure xml:id="fig-spe2-q3-region">
        <caption>
          The circle <m>r = 2\cos\theta</m> and the curve <m>r = 2\cos^2\theta</m> of <xref ref="spe2-q3"/>. They meet at the origin and at <m>(2,0)</m>, and in between the curve lies inside the circle. The shaded region, outside the curve and inside the circle, is swept by the rays <m>-\pi/2 \le \theta \le \pi/2</m>; along each, <m>r</m> runs from <m>2\cos^2\theta</m> to <m>2\cos\theta</m>.
        </caption>
        <image width="55%">
          <shortdescription>
          A circle of radius one centered at one comma zero, and inside it a narrower closed curve through the origin and the point two comma zero; the two crescents between them, above and below the x axis, are shaded.
          </shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>
            Coordinate axes x and y. A blue circle of radius one is centered at one comma zero, passing through the origin and the point two comma zero. An orange closed curve, r equals two cosine squared theta, also passes through those two points but bulges less, staying inside the circle. The two crescent-shaped regions between the curve and the circle, one above and one below the x axis, are shaded tan. A green ray from the origin at an angle theta crosses the orange curve and then the circle, with the two crossing points marked.
            </p>
          </description>
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-spe2-q3-region">
            <diagram dimensions="(350,280)" margins="10">
              <definition>circ(t) = (2*cos(t)*cos(t), 2*cos(t)*sin(t))</definition>
              <definition>curve(t) = (2*cos(t)^2*cos(t), 2*cos(t)^2*sin(t))</definition>
              <coordinates bbox="(-0.6,-1.4,2.9,1.4)">
              <polygon at="q3-region" points="((7.499e-33,-1.225e-16),(0.003805,-0.08716),(0.01519,-0.1736),(0.03407,-0.2588),(0.06031,-0.342),(0.09369,-0.4226),(0.134,-0.5),(0.1808,-0.5736),(0.234,-0.6428),(0.2929,-0.7071),(0.3572,-0.766),(0.4264,-0.8192),(0.5,-0.866),(0.5774,-0.9063),(0.658,-0.9397),(0.7412,-0.9659),(0.8264,-0.9848),(0.9128,-0.9962),(1,-1),(1.087,-0.9962),(1.174,-0.9848),(1.259,-0.9659),(1.342,-0.9397),(1.423,-0.9063),(1.5,-0.866),(1.574,-0.8192),(1.643,-0.766),(1.707,-0.7071),(1.766,-0.6428),(1.819,-0.5736),(1.866,-0.5),(1.906,-0.4226),(1.94,-0.342),(1.966,-0.2588),(1.985,-0.1736),(1.996,-0.08716),(2,0),(1.996,0.08716),(1.985,0.1736),(1.966,0.2588),(1.94,0.342),(1.906,0.4226),(1.866,0.5),(1.819,0.5736),(1.766,0.6428),(1.707,0.7071),(1.643,0.766),(1.574,0.8192),(1.5,0.866),(1.423,0.9063),(1.342,0.9397),(1.259,0.9659),(1.174,0.9848),(1.087,0.9962),(1,1),(0.9128,0.9962),(0.8264,0.9848),(0.7412,0.9659),(0.658,0.9397),(0.5774,0.9063),(0.5,0.866),(0.4264,0.8192),(0.3572,0.766),(0.2929,0.7071),(0.234,0.6428),(0.1808,0.5736),(0.134,0.5),(0.09369,0.4226),(0.06031,0.342),(0.03407,0.2588),(0.01519,0.1736),(0.003805,0.08716),(7.499e-33,1.225e-16),(4.592e-49,7.499e-33),(0.000166,0.003802),(0.001324,0.01513),(0.004448,0.03378),(0.01047,0.05939),(0.02028,0.09147),(0.03468,0.1294),(0.05438,0.1725),(0.08002,0.2198),(0.1121,0.2706),(0.151,0.3237),(0.1969,0.3782),(0.25,0.433),(0.3102,0.487),(0.3774,0.539),(0.4512,0.588),(0.5312,0.633),(0.6167,0.673),(0.7071,0.7071),(0.8015,0.7345),(0.8991,0.7544),(0.9987,0.7663),(1.099,0.7698),(1.2,0.7644),(1.299,0.75),(1.396,0.7266),(1.489,0.6943),(1.577,0.6533),(1.66,0.604),(1.735,0.547),(1.802,0.483),(1.861,0.4126),(1.91,0.3368),(1.949,0.2566),(1.977,0.173),(1.994,0.08707),(2,0),(1.994,-0.08707),(1.977,-0.173),(1.949,-0.2566),(1.91,-0.3368),(1.861,-0.4126),(1.802,-0.483),(1.735,-0.547),(1.66,-0.604),(1.577,-0.6533),(1.489,-0.6943),(1.396,-0.7266),(1.299,-0.75),(1.2,-0.7644),(1.099,-0.7698),(0.9987,-0.7663),(0.8991,-0.7544),(0.8015,-0.7345),(0.7071,-0.7071),(0.6167,-0.673),(0.5312,-0.633),(0.4512,-0.588),(0.3774,-0.539),(0.3102,-0.487),(0.25,-0.433),(0.1969,-0.3782),(0.151,-0.3237),(0.1121,-0.2706),(0.08002,-0.2198),(0.05438,-0.1725),(0.03468,-0.1294),(0.02028,-0.09147),(0.01047,-0.05939),(0.004448,-0.03378),(0.001324,-0.01513),(0.000166,-0.003802),(4.592e-49,-7.499e-33))" fill="#fbe3d0" stroke="none" closed="yes"/>
              <axes at="q3-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" hlabels="(1,1,1)" vlabels="(-1,1,1)" arrows="1"/>
              <parametric-curve at="q3-circle" function="circ" domain="(-pi/2,pi/2)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
              <parametric-curve at="q3-curve" function="curve" domain="(-pi/2,pi/2)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
              <line at="q3-ray" endpoints="((0,0),(1.5,0.866))" stroke="#009E73" thickness="2.5"/>
              <arc at="q3-angle" center="(0,0)" radius="0.45" range="(0,30)" stroke="#009E73" thickness="2"/>
              <point at="q3-in" p="(1.299,0.75)" fill="#D55E00" size="3.5"/>
              <point at="q3-out" p="(1.5,0.866)" fill="#0072B2" size="3.5"/>
              <point at="q3-two" p="(2,0)" fill="black" size="3.5"/>
              <label at="q3-thetalab" anchor="(0.55,0.12)" alignment="e" color="#009E73"><m>\theta</m></label>
              <label at="q3-circlab" anchor="(1.65,0.95)" alignment="ne" color="#0072B2"><m>r = 2\cos\theta</m></label>
              <label at="q3-curvelab" anchor="(1.0,-1.05)" alignment="s" offset="(0,-3)" color="#D55E00"><m>r = 2\cos^2\theta</m></label>
              <label at="q3-twolab" anchor="(2,0)" alignment="se" offset="(3,-4)"><m>(2,0)</m></label>
              </coordinates>
              <annotations>
              <annotation ref="figure" text="The circle r equals two cosine theta and the curve r equals two cosine squared theta, with the region between them shaded.">
                <annotation ref="q3-region" text="The shaded region, outside the curve and inside the circle."/>
                <annotation ref="q3-circle" text="The circle r equals two cosine theta, of radius one centered at one comma zero." sonify="yes"/>
                <annotation ref="q3-curve" text="The curve r equals two cosine squared theta, inside the circle." sonify="yes"/>
                <annotation ref="q3-ray" text="A ray at angle theta, which enters the region on the curve and leaves it on the circle."/>
              </annotation>
              </annotations>
            </diagram>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
      </figure>
      <p>
        For <m>-\pi/2 \le \theta \le \pi/2</m> we have
        <m>0 \le \cos\theta \le 1</m>, so <m>2\cos^2\theta \le 2\cos\theta</m>:
        along a ray at angle <m>\theta</m> the region runs from the curve
        out to the circle. With <m>dA = r\,dr\,d\theta</m> by
        <xref ref="eq-polar-area-element"/>, and using the symmetry about
        the <m>x</m>-axis,
      </p>
      <me>
        A = \int_{-\pi/2}^{\pi/2}\!\!\int_{2\cos^2\theta}^{2\cos\theta} r\,dr\,d\theta
        = 2\int_{0}^{\pi/2}\!\!\int_{2\cos^2\theta}^{2\cos\theta} r\,dr\,d\theta .
      </me>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ===================== Question 4 ===================== -->
  <!-- Question 1 of the Winter 2025 Exam 3. -->
  <exercise xml:id="spe2-q4">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Rewrite (do not evaluate) the following integral in spherical
        coordinates:
      </p>
      <me>
        \int_{-2}^{0}\!\!\int_{0}^{\sqrt{4-y^2}}\!\!
        \int_{-\sqrt{8-x^2-y^2}}^{-\sqrt{x^2+y^2}} xyz\,dz\,dx\,dy .
      </me>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <em>The projection.</em> The limits <m>-2 \le y \le 0</m>,
        <m>0 \le x \le \sqrt{4 - y^2}</m> describe the quarter of the disk
        <m>x^2 + y^2 \le 4</m> with <m>x \ge 0</m> and <m>y \le 0</m>,
        the fourth quadrant (<xref ref="fig-spe2-q4-proj"/>): the rays
        through it have <m>3\pi/2 \le \theta \le 2\pi</m>.
      </p>
      <figure xml:id="fig-spe2-q4-proj">
        <caption>
          The projection of the solid of <xref ref="spe2-q4"/> onto the <m>xy</m>-plane: the quarter of the disk <m>x^2 + y^2 \le 4</m> in the fourth quadrant, <m>-2 \le y \le 0</m> and <m>0 \le x \le \sqrt{4-y^2}</m>, swept by <m>3\pi/2 \le \theta \le 2\pi</m>.
        </caption>
        <image width="42%">
          <shortdescription>
          A shaded quarter disk of radius two in the fourth quadrant, bounded by the positive x axis, the negative y axis, and a quarter circle.
          </shortdescription>
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-spe2-q4-proj">
            <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="10">
              <definition>circ(t) = (2*cos(t), 2*sin(t))</definition>
              <coordinates bbox="(-0.9,-2.7,2.7,0.9)">
              <polygon at="q4p-region" points="((0,0),(1.225e-16,-2),(0.08724,-1.998),(0.1743,-1.992),(0.2611,-1.983),(0.3473,-1.97),(0.4329,-1.953),(0.5176,-1.932),(0.6014,-1.907),(0.684,-1.879),(0.7654,-1.848),(0.8452,-1.813),(0.9235,-1.774),(1,-1.732),(1.075,-1.687),(1.147,-1.638),(1.218,-1.587),(1.286,-1.532),(1.351,-1.475),(1.414,-1.414),(1.475,-1.351),(1.532,-1.286),(1.587,-1.218),(1.638,-1.147),(1.687,-1.075),(1.732,-1),(1.774,-0.9235),(1.813,-0.8452),(1.848,-0.7654),(1.879,-0.684),(1.907,-0.6014),(1.932,-0.5176),(1.953,-0.4329),(1.97,-0.3473),(1.983,-0.2611),(1.992,-0.1743),(1.998,-0.08724),(2,0))" fill="#fbe3d0" stroke="none" closed="yes"/>
              <axes at="q4p-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" hlabels="(1,1,1)" vlabels="(-2,1,-1)" arrows="1"/>
              <parametric-curve at="q4p-arc" function="circ" domain="(-pi/2,0)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
              <line at="q4p-ray" endpoints="((0,0),(1.4142,-1.4142))" stroke="#009E73" thickness="2.5"/>
              <arc at="q4p-angle" center="(0,0)" radius="0.5" range="(0,315)" stroke="#009E73" thickness="2"/>
              <point at="q4p-end" p="(1.4142,-1.4142)" fill="#009E73" size="3.5"/>
              <label at="q4p-thetalab" anchor="(-0.4,0.4)" alignment="nw" color="#009E73"><m>\theta</m></label>
              <label at="q4p-arclab" anchor="(1.55,-1.0)" alignment="ne" offset="(3,2)" color="#0072B2"><m>x^2+y^2=4</m></label>
              <label at="q4p-tlab" anchor="(0.08,-2.25)" alignment="e" color="#009E73"><m>\theta=\tfrac{3\pi}{2}</m></label>
              <label at="q4p-t2lab" anchor="(1.3,0.12)" alignment="ne" color="#009E73"><m>\theta=2\pi</m></label>
              </coordinates>
              <annotations>
              <annotation ref="figure" text="The quarter disk of radius two in the fourth quadrant, the shadow of the solid.">
                <annotation ref="q4p-region" text="The shaded quarter disk."/>
                <annotation ref="q4p-arc" text="The quarter circle of radius two from the positive x axis down to the negative y axis." sonify="yes"/>
                <annotation ref="q4p-ray" text="A ray from the origin at an angle theta between three pi over two and two pi."/>
              </annotation>
              </annotations>
            </diagram>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
      </figure>
      <p>
        <em>The solid.</em> The lower limit on <m>z</m>,
        <m>z = -\sqrt{8 - x^2 - y^2}</m>, is the lower half of the sphere
        <m>x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 8</m>, which is <m>\rho = 2\sqrt2</m>; the
        upper limit, <m>z = -\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}</m>, is the lower nappe of the
        cone <m>z^2 = x^2 + y^2</m>, which is <m>\phi = 3\pi/4</m> (see
        <xref ref="eq-spherical-to-rect"/>: on it
        <m>\rho\cos\phi = -\rho\sin\phi</m>, so <m>\tan\phi = -1</m> with
        <m>\phi</m> in the lower half). The solid lies below the cone and
        above the sphere, so it is the part of the ball <m>\rho \le 2\sqrt2</m>
        with <m>3\pi/4 \le \phi \le \pi</m>, an ice-cream cone pointing
        down, cut to the quarter <m>3\pi/2 \le \theta \le 2\pi</m>
        (<xref ref="fig-spe2-q4-solid"/>). As a check, the cone and the
        sphere meet where <m>x^2 + y^2 = 8 - (x^2 + y^2)</m>, that is, on
        the circle <m>x^2 + y^2 = 4</m>, which is exactly the boundary of
        the projection.
      </p>
      <figure xml:id="fig-spe2-q4-solid">
        <caption>
          The solid of <xref ref="spe2-q4"/>: the quarter, over the fourth quadrant, of the region inside the sphere <m>\rho = 2\sqrt2</m> and below the cone <m>\phi = 3\pi/4</m>. In spherical coordinates it is <m>3\pi/2 \le \theta \le 2\pi</m>, <m>3\pi/4 \le \phi \le \pi</m>, <m>0 \le \rho \le 2\sqrt2</m>.
        </caption>
        <image width="55%">
          <shortdescription>
          A quarter of a downward ice-cream cone: an orange cone with its vertex at the origin opening downward, capped below by a blue piece of a sphere, cut by the two vertical planes x equals zero and y equals zero so that only the part over the fourth quadrant remains.
          </shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>
            Coordinate axes x, y and z. Below the xy-plane, an orange conical surface descends from the origin at forty-five degrees from the negative z axis, and a blue spherical surface of radius two root two closes it from below; the two meet along a quarter circle of radius two at depth two. Two flat gray faces, in the planes x equals zero and y equals zero, cut the solid to the quarter over the fourth quadrant, where x is positive and y is negative.
            </p>
          </description>
          <latex-image>
            <![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[line join=round, line cap=round]
                      \definecolor{oiblue}{RGB}{0,114,178}
                      \definecolor{oiorange}{RGB}{213,94,0}
                      \definecolor{oigreen}{RGB}{0,158,115}
                      \begin{axis}[
                          view={40}{22},
                          width=10cm,
                          axis lines=center,
                          axis line style={-latex, black!70, line width=0.7pt},
                          xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
                          label style={font=\large},
                          ticks=none,
                          xmin=-1.2, xmax=3.2, ymin=-3.2, ymax=1.4, zmin=-3.3, zmax=1.2,
                          clip=false,
                          unit vector ratio=1 1 1,
                        ]
                        % the flat face in the plane x = 0 (theta = 3pi/2), and the one in y = 0 (theta = 2pi)
                        \addplot3[surf, shader=flat, color=black!12, opacity=0.5, fill opacity=0.5,
                                  domain=0:2.8284, samples=8, y domain=135:180, samples y=12,
                                  z buffer=sort, forget plot]
                          (0, {-x*sin(y)}, {x*cos(y)});
                        \addplot3[surf, shader=flat, color=black!12, opacity=0.5, fill opacity=0.5,
                                  domain=0:2.8284, samples=8, y domain=135:180, samples y=12,
                                  z buffer=sort, forget plot]
                          ({x*sin(y)}, 0, {x*cos(y)});
                        % the cone phi = 3pi/4, over the fourth quadrant
                        \addplot3[surf, shader=faceted, faceted color=oiorange!30,
                                  colormap={coneq4}{color=(oiorange!12!white) color=(oiorange!45!white)},
                                  opacity=0.6, fill opacity=0.6,
                                  domain=0:2, samples=10, y domain=270:360, samples y=16,
                                  z buffer=sort, forget plot]
                          ({x*cos(y)}, {x*sin(y)}, {-x});
                        % the sphere rho = 2 sqrt 2, phi from 3pi/4 to pi
                        \addplot3[surf, shader=faceted, faceted color=oiblue!30,
                                  colormap={sphq4}{color=(oiblue!15!white) color=(oiblue!55!white)},
                                  opacity=0.65, fill opacity=0.65,
                                  domain=135:180, samples=12, y domain=270:360, samples y=16,
                                  z buffer=sort, forget plot]
                          ({2.8284*sin(x)*cos(y)}, {2.8284*sin(x)*sin(y)}, {2.8284*cos(x)});
                        % the circle where they meet, and the straight edges
                        \addplot3[oiblue, line width=1.4pt, samples=40, samples y=0, domain=270:360, forget plot]
                          ({2*cos(x)}, {2*sin(x)}, -2);
                        \draw[oiorange!85!black, line width=1.2pt] (axis cs:0,0,0) -- (axis cs:2,0,-2);
                        \draw[oiorange!85!black, line width=1.2pt] (axis cs:0,0,0) -- (axis cs:0,-2,-2);
                        \addplot3[oiblue, line width=1.2pt, samples=30, samples y=0, domain=135:180, forget plot]
                          ({2.8284*sin(x)}, 0, {2.8284*cos(x)});
                        \addplot3[oiblue, line width=1.2pt, samples=30, samples y=0, domain=135:180, forget plot]
                          (0, {-2.8284*sin(x)}, {2.8284*cos(x)});
                        % the angle phi = 3pi/4, from the positive z-axis down to the cone's edge in the xz-plane
                        \addplot3[oigreen, line width=1pt, samples=40, samples y=0, domain=0:135, forget plot]
                          ({0.6*sin(x)}, 0, {0.6*cos(x)});
                        \node[oigreen!70!black, font=\small, anchor=west] at (axis cs:0.62,0,-0.12) {$\phi=\tfrac{3\pi}{4}$};
                        \node[oiorange!85!black, font=\small, anchor=west] at (axis cs:2.1,0,-1.0) {$z=-\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$};
                        \node[oiblue, font=\small, anchor=north] at (axis cs:1.0,-1.0,-2.6) {$\rho=2\sqrt2$};
                      \end{axis}
                    \end{tikzpicture}]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>
      <p>
        <em>The integral.</em> With <m>x = \rho\sin\phi\cos\theta</m>,
        <m>y = \rho\sin\phi\sin\theta</m>, <m>z = \rho\cos\phi</m>, the
        integrand is
        <me>
          xyz = \rho^3\sin^2\phi\cos\phi\,\sin\theta\cos\theta ,
        </me>
        and <m>dV = \rho^2\sin\phi\,d\rho\,d\phi\,d\theta</m> by
        <xref ref="eq-dv-spherical"/>. Hence
      </p>
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \int_{-2}^{0}\!\!\int_{0}^{\sqrt{4-y^2}}\!\!
          \int_{-\sqrt{8-x^2-y^2}}^{-\sqrt{x^2+y^2}} xyz\,dz\,dx\,dy
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \quad = \int_{3\pi/2}^{2\pi}\!\!\int_{3\pi/4}^{\pi}\!\!\int_{0}^{2\sqrt2}
          \rho^5\sin^3\phi\cos\phi\,\sin\theta\cos\theta\,d\rho\,d\phi\,d\theta .
        </mrow>
      </md>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ===================== Question 5 ===================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="spe2-q5">
    <introduction>
      <p>
        <alert>Without calculating the integrals</alert>, decide whether
        each of the following integrals is positive, negative, or zero.
        Let <m>S</m> be the solid bounded by <m>z = (x-2)^2</m>,
        <m>z = 2 - (x-2)^2</m>, <m>y = -1</m>, and <m>y = 1</m>. Remember
        to justify your responses.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="spe2-q5-a">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\displaystyle\iiint_S \sin^5(x-3)\,dV</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <alert>Negative.</alert> First locate <m>S</m>. The two
          parabolic cylinders meet where
          <m>(x-2)^2 = 2 - (x-2)^2</m>, that is, <m>(x-2)^2 = 1</m>, so at
          <m>x = 1</m> and <m>x = 3</m>. Between them the second lies above
          the first, so
          <me>
            S:\quad 1 \le x \le 3,\qquad -1 \le y \le 1,\qquad (x-2)^2 \le z \le 2-(x-2)^2 ,
          </me>
          a solid with the cross-section of <xref ref="fig-spe2-q5-section"/>
          in every plane <m>y = c</m>, <m>-1 \le c \le 1</m>.
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-spe2-q5-section">
          <caption>
            The cross-section of the solid <m>S</m> of <xref ref="spe2-q5"/> by a plane <m>y = c</m>: the region between the parabolas <m>z = (x-2)^2</m> and <m>z = 2 - (x-2)^2</m>, which meet at <m>x = 1</m> and <m>x = 3</m>. It is symmetric about the dashed line <m>x = 2</m>.
          </caption>
          <image width="50%">
            <shortdescription>
            Two parabolas, one opening upward with vertex at two comma zero and one opening downward with vertex at two comma two, crossing at x equals one and x equals three; the lens between them is shaded and a dashed vertical line marks x equals two.
            </shortdescription>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-spe2-q5-section">
              <diagram dimensions="(382,200)" margins="10">
                <definition>lower(x) = (x-2)^2</definition>
                <definition>upper(x) = 2 - (x-2)^2</definition>
                <definition>lc(t) = (t, (t-2)^2)</definition>
                <definition>uc(t) = (t, 2 - (t-2)^2)</definition>
                <coordinates bbox="(-0.35,-0.4,5.0,2.4)">
                <area-between-curves at="q5-region" function1="lower" function2="upper" domain="(1,3)" fill="#fbe3d0" stroke="none"/>
                <axes at="q5-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="z" hlabels="(1,1,3)" vlabels="(1,1,2)" arrows="1"/>
                <parametric-curve at="q5-lower" function="lc" domain="(0.6,3.4)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
                <parametric-curve at="q5-upper" function="uc" domain="(0.6,3.4)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
                <line at="q5-axis" endpoints="((2,-0.3),(2,2.3))" stroke="#555555" thickness="1.5" dash="5 4"/>
                <point at="q5-p1" p="(1,1)" fill="black" size="3.5"/>
                <point at="q5-p3" p="(3,1)" fill="black" size="3.5"/>
                <label at="q5-lowlab" anchor="(3.2,1.45)" alignment="e" offset="(3,0)" color="#0072B2"><m>z=(x-2)^2</m></label>
                <label at="q5-uplab" anchor="(3.25,0.55)" alignment="e" offset="(3,0)" color="#D55E00"><m>z=2-(x-2)^2</m></label>
                <label at="q5-symlab" anchor="(2,2.3)" alignment="n" offset="(0,3)" color="#555555"><m>x=2</m></label>
                </coordinates>
                <annotations>
                <annotation ref="figure" text="The lens between an upward parabola with vertex at two comma zero and a downward parabola with vertex at two comma two, which cross at x equals one and x equals three.">
                  <annotation ref="q5-region" text="The shaded cross-section of the solid S."/>
                  <annotation ref="q5-lower" text="The parabola z equals x minus two squared, the floor of S." sonify="yes"/>
                  <annotation ref="q5-upper" text="The parabola z equals two minus x minus two squared, the roof of S." sonify="yes"/>
                  <annotation ref="q5-axis" text="The line x equals two, about which the cross-section is symmetric."/>
                </annotation>
                </annotations>
              </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
        </figure>
        <p>
          On <m>S</m> we have <m>1 \le x \le 3</m>, so <m>-2 \le x - 3 \le 0</m>. On the
          interval <m>[-2, 0]</m>, which lies inside <m>[-\pi, 0]</m>, the
          sine is at most <m>0</m>, so <m>\sin(x-3) \le 0</m> and therefore
          <m>\sin^5(x-3) \le 0</m> throughout <m>S</m>, with equality only
          on the face <m>x = 3</m>. An integrand that is negative on the
          solid except on a face of zero volume has a negative integral.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="spe2-q5-b">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\displaystyle\iiint_S \sin^3(x-2)\,dV</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <alert>Zero.</alert> The solid, described in the solution of
          part (a), is symmetric about the plane <m>x = 2</m>
          (<xref ref="fig-spe2-q5-section"/>): replacing <m>x - 2</m> by <m>-(x-2)</m> changes
          none of its bounding surfaces, so the reflection
          <m>x \mapsto 4 - x</m> maps <m>S</m> onto itself. The integrand
          is odd under the same reflection,
          <me>
            \sin^3\bigl((4-x) - 2\bigr) = \sin^3\bigl(-(x-2)\bigr) = -\sin^3(x-2):
          </me>
          it is <m>\ge 0</m> on the half <m>x \ge 2</m> of <m>S</m> (there
          <m>0 \le x - 2 \le 1</m>) and <m>\le 0</m> on the half
          <m>x \le 2</m>, with the values on the two halves mirror images
          of each other. The two halves contribute opposite amounts and the
          integral is <m>0</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ===================== Question 6 ===================== -->
  <!-- The instructor's true/false problem on triple integrals. -->
  <exercise xml:id="spe2-q6">
    <statement>
      <p>
        <alert>True or False?</alert> If the statement is true, prove it;
        if it is false, give a counterexample.
      </p>
      <p>
        Let <m>E</m> be the solid unit ball
        <m>E = \{(x,y,z) : x^2 + y^2 + z^2 \le 1\}</m>. Then
      </p>
      <me>
        \iiint_E \frac{x^2}{x^2 + y^2 + z^2}\,dV = \frac13\operatorname{Vol}(E) .
      </me>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <alert>True.</alert> Write
        <me>
          I_x = \iiint_E \frac{x^2}{x^2 + y^2 + z^2}\,dV ,
        </me>
        and define <m>I_y</m> and <m>I_z</m> in the same way, with
        <m>y^2</m> and <m>z^2</m> in the numerator.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Use symmetry.</em> The unit ball is unchanged when the
        coordinate axes are permuted. Swapping <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>, that
        is, the change of variables <m>(x,y,z) \mapsto (y,x,z)</m>, maps
        <m>E</m> onto itself, does not change volumes (its Jacobian
        determinant is <m>-1</m>), and turns the integrand of <m>I_x</m>
        into that of <m>I_y</m>, since the denominator
        <m>x^2 + y^2 + z^2</m> is unchanged. So <m>I_x = I_y</m>, and
        swapping <m>x</m> and <m>z</m> in the same way gives
        <m>I_x = I_z</m>:
        <me>
          I_x = I_y = I_z .
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Add the three equal integrals.</em>
        <md>
          <mrow>
            I_x + I_y + I_z \amp = \iiint_E \frac{x^2 + y^2 + z^2}{x^2 + y^2 + z^2}\,dV
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp = \iiint_E 1\,dV = \operatorname{Vol}(E) .
          </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Conclude.</em> Since the three integrals are equal,
        <me>
          3I_x = \operatorname{Vol}(E)
          \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
          I_x = \frac13\operatorname{Vol}(E) .
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Numerical value.</em> Since
        <m>\operatorname{Vol}(E) = \tfrac43\pi</m>, the integral is
        <m>\tfrac13\cdot\tfrac43\pi = \tfrac{4\pi}{9}</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
</worksheet>
